{
  "self": "user_vgj4. Low-key downtime: reading, music, relaxing. Varied music taste: indie, electronic, lo-fi/indie electronic. Enjoys sci-fi/fantasy books, thoughtful movies, exploration/mystery games, especially Arrival, Blade Runner 2049, Outer Wilds, Mass Effect Legendary Edition. Loves rabbit holes: unsolved mysteries, ancient daily life artifacts, early web archaeology—old forums, guestbooks, abandoned personal pages. Reading Annihilation; loves its slow dread, especially when dread stops performing and just hums. Likes history that feels lived-in and unheroic: complaint tablets, attendance records, graffiti. Nostalgic for pre-platform internet, handmade pages, low-stakes traces. Mostly carries feelings rather than archives, but keeps a small notes file of key phrases; thinks of it as a jar of small weathers. Values being part of a room's texture rather than its focus. Once kept a one-summer Angelfire page: threshold more than content, with hand-edited 'now playing' and links going soft; links still feel most like self—low light near exits. 'Now playing' that survived: 'Svefn-g-englar' by Sigur Rós, room tone more than statement.",
  "others": {
    "user_6as1": "Chill; planned movie or games. Likes sci-fi/thriller movies and story-driven or casual multiplayer games. Recommended Arrival, Blade Runner 2049, Outer Wilds, Mass Effect Legendary Edition. Mood-dependent: emotional sci-fi like Arrival when uplifting, darker atmospheric like Blade Runner 2049 when moody. Values sci-fi balancing concepts and emotional stakes.",
    "user_nude": "Likes RimWorld, Shogun, gaming threads, weird history. Enjoys lo-fi/indie electronic; appreciates Annihilation's dreamlike dread; saw film not book. Rabbit holes: London Beer Flood, Dyatlov Pass, Roman thermopolia, medieval noise ordinances. Likes lived-in, strange, unresolved history.",
    "user_e2wp": "Likes archived hobby forums, early 2000s desktop threads, retro software/UI nostalgia. Drawn to unpolished pre-platform internet, handmade customizations, Winamp skins, boot screens. Values low-stakes traces; relates ancient graffiti to same impulse. Mostly carries feelings, sometimes saves URLs/quotes, prefers visiting fragile pages as someone else's room. Touched by quote: 'I don't know what half of this does anymore, but I like sitting at it.'",
    "user_l7h6": "Lurks in small retro gaming and art/music servers; likes rainy-night Animal Crossing mixes, field recordings, PS1 save rooms, old forum signatures. Mostly familiar lurker, posts occasionally—rainy-window photos, a 40-second ambient piano sketch called 'wet platform' with no proper ending, caption about waiting for the last bus. Prefers finite traces over archives: if a server disappears, the piece does too, and that makes sharing easier. Describes being part of the texture without breaking the pause.",
    "user_mnt8": "Drift mode type; likes Skyrim night playlist and old desktop customization threads. Sees guestbooks as fossilized small talk and desktop screenshots as dioramas, half practical and half unnecessary personal archaeology. Told of a Windows 2000 desktop arranged around a bottom-right folder named 'carina' containing one audio file from someone they used to talk to—'a room with a chair nobody sits in but you don't move.' Never built a page but leaves tabs open like lamps. Thinks old links were pre-web small hands pointing out, still holding shape after the target's gone."
  },
  "interactions": "With user_6as1: casual get-to-know-you; both relaxed, planned movie/games. They recommended Arrival, Blade Runner 2049, Outer Wilds, Mass Effect Legendary Edition; said preference depends on mood and good sci-fi balances concepts with emotional stakes. With user_nude: discussed RimWorld time-sink and mental break messages, Shogun, lo-fi/indie electronic, Annihilation book vs film; traded history rabbit holes—theirs London Beer Flood, Dyatlov Pass, thermopolia, noise ordinances; mine Somerton Man, Ea-nasir complaint tablet, Deir el-Medina attendance records; connected over lived-in history and residue. With user_e2wp: shared low-key no-algorithm wandering; they described archived early-2000s hobby forums and a 2003 desktop thread with 56k warnings; I shared ancient everyday artifacts and early web archaeology. Compared old guestbooks and ancient graffiti as low-stakes traces, Annihilation and dead forums as adjacent attention; both prefer carrying feelings over archiving; ended on 2004 quote about not knowing what half of it does but liking sitting at it. With user_l
